# Onion architecture

Follow **ports and adapters** (onion / hexagonal) layering. Dependencies point **inward** only.

## Architectural roles, not folder names

The terms **core**, **application**, **adapter**, and **infrastructure** describe responsibilities and dependency direction. They do **not** require directories with those names. In particular, "infrastructure" does not mean that technology-specific code must live in a folder named `infrastructure/`.

A deployable app may contain its UI framework, HTTP handlers, database adapters, and composition root in whatever folder structure best fits that technology. A package may also contain an adapter when it is intentionally shared. Classify code by what it knows and depends on, not by its path:

- Code that expresses business rules without knowing how data is stored, transported, or displayed is **inner/core code**.
- Code that coordinates a business operation through core-defined ports is **application/use-case code**.
- Code that translates between a technology and a core contract is an **adapter**.
- Code that selects concrete implementations and wires them together is the **composition root**.

Folder names may communicate these roles, but folder placement alone never makes a dependency Onion-compliant. The decisive rule is that source-code dependencies point toward business policy: outer code may import inner code, while inner code must not import outer code.

## Coding guidelines

- Keep **business logic plain** in core: deterministic, framework-agnostic code with no transport, persistence, or UI concerns.
- Express integrations as **ports (interfaces)** owned by the inner layer that needs the capability; define domain-oriented method names instead of technology-oriented APIs.
- Put all **technology-specific implementation** behind adapters (DB, HTTP, queue, cache, filesystem, UI frameworks, SDKs), regardless of the adapters' folder names.
- Make implementations **depend on core contracts**; core must never depend on implementation packages.
- Keep mapping at boundaries: convert framework/ORM/request models to domain types in adapters before calling core use cases.
- Keep side effects outside core: orchestrate I/O in adapters and composition roots, not inside entities or use cases.
- Test core with fast unit tests (no network/files/DB); test adapters with integration tests against real technologies.

## Layers

1. **Core (typically shared code under `packages/`)** — Domain models, use cases, invariants, and **port interfaces** (abstract contracts for persistence, messaging, external services). Pure business logic; no knowledge of how I/O is done.

2. **Adapters (commonly inside `apps/` or shared adapter packages)** — Concrete implementations of ports: database repositories, HTTP handlers, queue consumers, file storage, UI components, and third-party SDK wrappers. Responsible for translating between a technology and the core. No particular directory name is required.

3. **Composition root (inside each deployable app)** — Wire core use cases to adapter implementations at startup (dependency injection). It belongs near application startup, whether or not a `composition`, `bootstrap`, or `infrastructure` folder exists.

## Dependency rules (non-negotiable)

- **Shared packages must not import from deployable apps.** Apps are composition boundaries, not reusable dependencies.
- **Core modules must not import** adapter modules, database drivers, ORMs, HTTP/UI frameworks, cloud SDKs, filesystem APIs, or other concrete I/O libraries.
- **Adapters depend on core**, not the reverse. Core defines interfaces; adapters implement them.
- When adding a new external dependency, default to placing it in an adapter layer, not in core.
- Do not infer dependency direction from directories. Verify imports and ownership of interfaces instead.

A package under `packages/` is not automatically core. A deliberately shared technology adapter may live there, but it must be identified as an adapter, depend inward on the relevant core package, and remain replaceable. Conversely, moving framework code into a folder named `core` does not make it inner-layer code.

## What belongs where

| Inner business policy | Outer technology-specific code |
|---|---|
| `Decision`, `Fund`, `Spend` entities | SQL/NoSQL repository implementations |
| Authorization and fund-allocation rules | REST/GraphQL route handlers |
| Use cases (`RecordSpend`, `ApproveDecision`) | Message queue publishers/consumers |
| Port interfaces (`DecisionRepository`, `AuditLog`) | Framework middleware, config, wiring |
| Domain values and errors | React components and view-model mapping |

## When scaffolding

- New **domain concept or rule** → `packages/<domain-or-use-case>`.
- New **deployable surface or I/O** → normally `apps/<name>` or a deliberately shared adapter package that implements core ports (never the other way around). It does not need an `infrastructure/` directory.
- If core needs data, add or extend a **port interface** in core; implement it in an adapter.

When requirements are ambiguous, choose the option that **keeps business logic testable without I/O** and **prevents leakage of implementation details into core**.
